	<body>
		<Nav active="services" />

		<main>
			<section class="page-hero">
				<div class="container">
					<h1>GMAX Compliance Checklist</h1>
					<p>A practical walkthrough for athletic directors: when to test, what the numbers mean, and what to do about a bad result.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="checklist-section">
				<div class="container">
					<div class="intro-card">
						<p>GMAX testing measures how much impact energy a playing surface absorbs — it's the standard proxy for concussion and injury risk on athletic fields. A lower GMAX reading means the surface absorbs more impact; a higher reading means more of that impact energy transfers to the athlete. For a deeper explanation of the scoring itself, see <a href="/what-does-gmax-score-mean">What Does a GMAX Score Mean?</a> This page is the compliance-planning side: what to actually do, and when.</p>
					</div>

					<article class="checklist-body">
						<h2><Icon name="clock" size={26} /> Testing Frequency</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Test at least annually for any field in regular athletic use</li>
							<li>Test before the start of each major sport season if the field sees year-round multi-sport use</li>
							<li>Test after any significant infill work, repair, or renovation</li>
							<li>Test after severe weather events that could have compacted or displaced infill (hail, prolonged heavy rain, extreme heat)</li>
							<li>Check your conference, UIL classification, and insurance carrier for any testing frequency they specifically require — this checklist is a general baseline, not a substitute for your governing body's rules</li>
						</ul>

						<h2><Icon name="gauge" size={26} /> Reading the Score</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Readings are typically taken at multiple points across the field, not just one location — a field can have localized problem areas even if the overall average looks fine</li>
							<li>Most athletic programs treat a GMAX reading below 200g as within normal range</li>
							<li>Readings approaching or exceeding 200g are generally flagged for corrective action</li>
							<li>Readings approaching 300g are generally treated as a safety concern requiring prompt attention — check your specific governing body's threshold, as exact cutoffs vary by organization</li>
							<li>A single high reading in one spot usually points to localized infill compaction; high readings across the whole field usually point to a broader infill or backing issue</li>
						</ul>

						<h2><Icon name="shield-check" size={26} /> Before Your Test</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Confirm your tester is using a calibrated device and following ASTM F1936 (the standard test method for impact attenuation of turf playing systems) or your governing body's specified method</li>
							<li>Have prior test results on hand for comparison — a trend matters more than a single data point</li>
							<li>Note any recent weather events, heavy-use periods, or repairs since the last test</li>
							<li>Identify known problem areas (goal mouths, high-traffic zones) so the tester can prioritize those points</li>
						</ul>

						<h2><Icon name="clipboard-check" size={26} /> After a High Reading: Corrective Actions</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Infill redistribution or top-up in the affected area — often resolves localized compaction issues</li>
							<li>Deep decompaction/aeration of the infill layer</li>
							<li>Re-test the corrected area to confirm the reading has come back into range before returning the field to full use</li>
							<li>If readings stay elevated after infill correction, the issue may be in the shock pad or backing — that's a bigger conversation about repair versus replacement, not a routine maintenance fix</li>
						</ul>

						<h2><Icon name="book-open" size={26} /> Documentation to Keep on File</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Dated GMAX test reports, including per-point readings, not just the average</li>
							<li>Records of any corrective action taken after a flagged reading, and the re-test results confirming it was resolved</li>
							<li>Testing method and equipment used (for insurance and liability purposes)</li>
							<li>A running maintenance log — testing records are far more useful as evidence of due diligence when there's a documented maintenance history behind them</li>
						</ul>
					</article>
				</div>
			</section>

